{
  "0": "Interest aggregation: replace the final-state readout with 0.8 times the last valid GRU state plus 0.2 times a masked, recency-weighted mean of valid GRU states, using weights proportional to exp(-distance_from_end/10). This may retain persistent interests while preserving recent intent. Keep history selection, embeddings, GRU topology, scoring, loss and optimizer fixed. Apply identically during training and prediction; retain one model per category.",
  "1": "Learning objective: add a pairwise margin term with weight 0.1 to the existing loss: mean softplus(1 + negative_score - positive_score), using only positive/negative pairs already present in each training batch. Encouraging explicit separation from sampled competitors may improve HR@10. Preserve sampling, features, embeddings, GRU topology, readout, scoring, optimizer, scheduler and batch/epoch settings; retain one model per category.",
  "2": "Network architecture: add a residual path around the GRU stack, combining each recurrent output with 0.2 times its corresponding input embedding before the existing readout. Use a bias-free projection only if dimensions differ. This may preserve item identity when recurrent updates oversmooth short histories. Keep features, embedding design, history selection, readout rule, scoring, loss and optimizer fixed. Update the predictor writer consistently; retain one model per category.",
  "3": "Embedding representation: normalize each nonpadding input item embedding by its RMS across embedding coordinates, with epsilon 1e-6 and one jointly learned scalar initialized to the initial embedding RMS. Keep padding exactly zero and leave candidate embeddings unchanged. Controlling input magnitude may reduce GRU gate saturation and improve generalization. Preserve embedding dimensions, features, GRU topology, history policy, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer. Mirror normalization in prediction; retain one model per category."
}
